from rb import *

def build_ch3(b):
    b.chapter(3, 'System Requirement Specification')
    b.p('This chapter analyses the operating environment of an emergency operations centre and specifies the functional and non-functional requirements of the platform, followed by its hardware and software configuration.')
    b.h2('3.1 System Analysis')
    b.p('During a flash flood an EOC receives a large volume of rapidly changing information: rainfall and water levels, citizen reports with locations and photographs, calls from field squads, and questions about shelter capacity. These must be combined into decisions about which incident to deal with first, which squad to send and which public warning to issue. The system therefore has to do four things reliably: keep a single shared picture on a map, apply fixed safety rules that no one can skip by accident, record who did what, and protect personal data and access.')
    b.p('Two kinds of users exist. The public (guests, citizens and volunteers) needs information and a safe way to report. Professionals (rescue officers, operators, commanders, administrators and developers) need operational tools, each limited to their responsibility. Administrators and developers hold identity and diagnostic permissions only; they never dispatch, triage, approve or publish alerts. Table 3.1 lists the roles and their responsibilities.')
    b.tbl('User Roles and Responsibilities', ['Role', 'Workspace', 'Responsibilities in the system'], [
        ['Guest', 'Public portal', 'Read flood watch, shelters, alerts and the forecasting scenario tool; report an emergency with limited rate; track a report by reference and code.'],
        ['Citizen', 'Public portal', 'As guest, plus own reports list, account security, optional MFA, verified contact details.'],
        ['Volunteer', 'Public portal', 'As citizen, plus volunteer verification status; assignments only after verification.'],
        ['Rescue officer', 'Console', 'See assigned missions, update mission status, record rescue outcomes, view alerts, twin and facilities.'],
        ['Operator', 'Console', 'Verify and manage incidents, run triage, manage volunteers and facilities, publish alerts; cannot approve gated dispatches.'],
        ['Commander', 'Console', 'All operator functions, plus approval or decline of gated dispatches (recent MFA), downgrade authorisation and audit log.'],
        ['Administrator', 'Console', 'Manage users, agencies and invitations, read the audit log, view settings; no operational permissions.'],
        ['Developer', 'Console', 'Diagnostics and the Data & Models view only.']], widths=[1.8, 1.7, 7.5], size=9.5, bold_first=True)
    b.h2('3.2 Functional Requirements')
    b.p('Table 3.2 lists the functional requirements with their specification. Each requirement is covered by at least one automated or scripted test (Chapter 8).')
    FR = [['FR-01', 'Public flood information', 'Home, Flood Digital Twin, Forecasting, Alerts and Facilities pages open without an account; every figure carries a provenance label.'],
          ['FR-02', 'Emergency reporting', 'Category, severity, description, location (preset, device or map click) and optional photo; receipt with reference and one-time tracking code shown only after the server confirms.'],
          ['FR-03', 'Report tracking', 'A report can be followed by reference and tracking code; the code is stored only as a hash.'],
          ['FR-04', 'Private evidence', 'Photos are stored outside the public web root; retrieval needs authentication; anonymous requests receive 401.'],
          ['FR-05', 'Photo analysis', 'CLIP flood decision and SegFormer water coverage; result labelled PREDICTED and confirmed by an operator.'],
          ['FR-06', 'Incident lifecycle', 'Thirteen states with an allowed-transition table; illegal transitions return 409.'],
          ['FR-07', 'Triage', 'Nearest AVAILABLE squad and nearest shelter that can take all victims (Haversine distance); recommendation is advisory.'],
          ['FR-08', 'Commander approval gate', 'Five or more victims, or HIGH or CRITICAL severity, requires commander approval; operators receive 403.'],
          ['FR-09', 'Atomic dispatch', 'Squad DEPLOYED and incident DISPATCHED in one transaction; one team per incident.'],
          ['FR-10', 'Flood-risk estimate', 'Probability, risk class (LOW, MODERATE, HIGH, CRITICAL), advisory, contributing factors and scenario projections for user-set inputs.'],
          ['FR-11', 'Alerts', 'Create, acknowledge and resolve alerts; export CAP 1.2 XML; suppress duplicates; escalate overdue alerts.'],
          ['FR-12', 'Volunteers and squads', 'Registration, verification, availability, assignment; only verified volunteers can be assigned.'],
          ['FR-13', 'Facilities', 'Hospitals, shelters, relief camps, stations with capacity, occupancy and distance.'],
          ['FR-14', 'Rescue records', 'Record outcomes of each mission and export them as CSV.'],
          ['FR-15', 'Authentication', 'Password sign-in, TOTP challenge, recovery codes, password recovery, contact verification, session revocation.'],
          ['FR-16', 'Authorisation', 'Role-to-permission matrix enforced on every endpoint; the server supplies navigation and capabilities.'],
          ['FR-17', 'Invitations and agencies', 'Privileged accounts exist only through invitations or host-authorised bootstrap; agencies are metadata.'],
          ['FR-18', 'Audit log', 'Administrative, security and dispatch events are recorded with actor, role, target and time.'],
          ['FR-19', 'Mapping', 'Four visual presets, light and dark map appearance, clustering, search, legend, fullscreen, offline fallback.'],
          ['FR-20', 'Diagnostics', 'Live checks of the service, database, model files and optional components.']]
    b.tbl('Functional Requirements Specification', ['ID', 'Requirement', 'Specification and pass criteria'], FR, widths=[1, 2.4, 7.6], size=9.5)
    b.h2('3.3 Non-Functional Requirements')
    b.bul(['**Security:** central permission matrix, server-side sessions, MFA for staff, hashed secrets, CSRF protection for cookie-based refresh, private evidence, parameterised queries through the object-relational mapper.',
           '**Privacy:** collect and show personal and location data only to roles that need it; guest report limits; tracking codes and tokens stored as hashes.',
           '**Reliability:** atomic database transactions, migrations that are tested for upgrade, downgrade and automatic pre-migration backup, a deterministic rule-based fallback when no language model is available.',
           '**Explainability and auditability:** show the factors behind a risk estimate and keep a traceable log of verification, dispatch and approval.',
           '**Data labelling:** every record is SIMULATED, HISTORICAL or OBSERVED; no record is labelled LIVE without a real feed.',
           '**Performance:** model prediction and nearest-asset queries complete in milliseconds on a laptop; the photo analysis takes about 190 ms per photo on CPU.',
           '**Usability and accessibility:** responsive layouts from 360 px to 1920 px, light and dark themes, keyboard-reachable controls, colour never the only signal.',
           '**Maintainability:** modular routers and services, typed schemas (Pydantic v2, TypeScript) and automated tests for each module.',
           '**Portability:** runs on one machine without paid services; setup documented for Windows.'])
    b.h2('3.4 Hardware and Software Configuration')
    b.tbl('Hardware Requirements', ['Component', 'Minimum', 'Recommended'], [['Processor', 'Dual-core 2 GHz, 64-bit', 'Quad-core or better (development laptop: Intel Core i5, 12 threads)'], ['Memory', '4 GB', '8 GB or more'], ['Storage', '2 GB free', '5 GB free (models, uploads, backups)'], ['Network', 'Local network', 'Internet access for online map tiles (optional)'], ['Display', '360 px wide', '1366 px or wider for the console'], ['GPU', 'Not required', 'Not used']], widths=[2, 3, 5], size=9.5, bold_first=True)
    b.tbl('Software Requirements', ['Layer', 'Software'], [['Operating system', 'Linux, Windows 10/11 or macOS'], ['Back end', 'Python 3.13, FastAPI, Uvicorn, Pydantic v2, SQLAlchemy 2, Alembic'], ['Machine learning', 'scikit-learn (Random Forest), NumPy, joblib, ONNX Runtime, Pillow'], ['Security libraries', 'PyJWT, pyotp, segno, cryptography (AES-256-GCM)'], ['Front end', 'Node.js, React 19, TypeScript, Vite, Leaflet, leaflet.markercluster, lucide-react'], ['Database', 'SQLite (Alembic revisions 0001 to 0006)'], ['Testing', 'pytest, Vitest with Testing Library, Playwright (scripted browser runs)'], ['Browser', 'Current Chromium, Firefox or Safari']], widths=[2.4, 7.2], size=9.5, bold_first=True)

def build_ch4(b):
    b.chapter(4, 'Methodology')
    b.p('This chapter explains how each analytical and protective component of the platform works: the data and flood-risk model, the scenario projections, the geospatial methods, photo analysis, the approval gate, alert rules, the security mechanisms and the map-source strategy.')
    b.h2('4.1 Overview of the Processing Pipeline')
    b.p('The platform processes two streams in parallel. The flood-risk stream turns user-set or observed conditions into a risk class and scenario projections. The incident stream turns reports into verified incidents, proposals and dispatches under a safety gate. Figure 4.1 shows the flood-risk stream; the incident stream is described in Section 4.7 and in Chapter 6.')
    b.fig('fig/f_mlpipe.png', 'Flood-risk pipeline from inputs to risk class and scenario projections', 14.2)
    b.h2('4.2 Dataset and Input Validation')
    b.p('The flood-risk model is trained on the tabular dataset supplied with the project, **clean_flood_prediction_data.csv**, which holds 1,000 hourly records with four measurements: rainfall (mm per hour), water level (m), relative humidity (%) and temperature (degrees Celsius), and a binary flood label. Of the 1,000 records, 784 (78.4 %) are non-flood and 216 (21.6 %) are flood. The collection method of this dataset is not independently verified in this work, so all model figures in this report describe this dataset only.')
    b.p('An audit of the data showed that the label is defined as **water level above 4.21 m**. A model that sees the water level can therefore reproduce the label exactly without learning anything about weather. For this reason the served model uses only the three weather inputs, and the water level is applied afterwards as a separate and transparent gauge rule (Section 4.4). Inputs entering the API are validated by schema bounds: rainfall 0 to 400 mm/h, water level 0 to 25 m, humidity 0 to 100 % and temperature minus 10 to 60 degrees Celsius; values outside these bounds are rejected with a validation error.')
    b.p('Geographic data comes from the IndoFloods collection that accompanies the project: catchment shapefiles and a metadata table of gauge stations. The Karnataka stations are read from the metadata table, giving 23 gauge stations with their warning and danger levels, and the catchment polygons displayed in the Karnataka view number five. These are **historical reference data**; no live gauge reading is connected.')
    b.h2('4.3 Random Forest Classification')
    b.p('Flood probability is estimated with a Random Forest of 100 trees, maximum depth 6, balanced class weights and a fixed random seed of 42. One hundred trees give a stable ensemble, the depth limit keeps individual trees interpretable and guards against overfitting a small dataset, and balanced class weights compensate for the 78.4 to 21.6 class imbalance. The data is split 80/20 with stratification, giving 800 training and 200 hold-out records. For an input vector x, each tree b returns a flood probability and the forest averages them, as given in Equation 4.1.')
    b.eq('P(flood | x) = (1 / B) × Σ_b  p_b(x),    B = 100', '4.1')
    b.p('Each tree is grown by choosing at every node the split that most reduces the Gini impurity, defined in Equation 4.2, where p_k is the class-weighted share of class k in the node. The importance of a feature is the total impurity reduction it provides, averaged over the trees and normalised to 100 %.')
    b.eq('G = 1 − Σ_k  p_k²', '4.2')
    b.p('The classification quality is reported with the standard measures of Equations 4.3 to 4.5, where TP, TN, FP and FN are the counts of the confusion matrix.')
    b.eq('Accuracy = (TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)', '4.3')
    b.eq('Precision = TP / (TP + FP),     Recall = TP / (TP + FN)', '4.4')
    b.eq('F1 = 2 × Precision × Recall / (Precision + Recall)', '4.5')
    b.h2('4.4 Risk Classes and the Gauge Rule')
    b.p('The averaged probability P is mapped to a risk class, and the water level w, entered or measured, can independently raise it. The final class is the higher of the two results (Table 4.1). Because the gauge rule is plain code, an operator can see why a class was chosen, and a high water level is never hidden by a low weather-based probability.')
    b.tbl('Risk Classes from Model Probability and Gauge Rule', ['Class', 'Model probability P', 'Water level w', 'Meaning'], [
        ['CRITICAL', 'P ≥ 0.75', 'w ≥ 5.5 m', 'Severe inundation likely; immediate low-lying evacuation and dispatch recommended'],
        ['HIGH', 'P ≥ 0.50', 'w ≥ 3.8 m', 'High flood probability; alert disaster management teams and stage assets'],
        ['MODERATE', 'P ≥ 0.25', 'w ≥ 2.5 m', 'Elevated runoff conditions; continuous monitoring required'],
        ['LOW', 'P < 0.25', 'w < 2.5 m', 'Within normal drainage capacity; standard monitoring']], widths=[2.0, 2.4, 2.1, 5.7], size=9.5, bold_first=True)
    b.h2('4.5 Scenario Projections for the Next Hour')
    b.p('To show how risk might develop over the next hour, the system applies a fixed runoff-surge adjustment to the current model probability. With rainfall intensity i in mm per hour, the surge s and the projection at horizon t (30, 45 or 60 minutes) are given by Equations 4.6 and 4.7, with the coefficient k_t equal to 0.5, 0.9 and 1.3 for the three horizons, and the result limited to the range 0.01 to 0.99.')
    b.eq('s = (i / 100) × 0.15', '4.6')
    b.eq('P(t) = min( 0.99, max( 0.01, P + k_t × s ) )', '4.7')
    b.p('These values are **heuristic scenario projections**. They are not outputs of a trained forecasting model, they do not use sensor time series, and they are not validated forecasts. An experiment on the dataset (Chapter 8) showed that predicting the next hour from the current hour is close to chance, because the rows are nearly independent. The water level does not enter Equations 4.6 and 4.7; it affects only the risk class through the gauge rule. Table 4.2 shows worked examples computed by the running service.')
    b.tbl('Worked Examples of the Risk Estimate (values computed by the served model)', ['Scenario (rain mm/h, water m, humidity %, temp C)', 'P', 'Risk class', '+30 min', '+45 min', '+60 min'], [
        ['Light monsoon (8, 1.8, 65, 30)', '0.054', 'LOW', '0.060', '0.065', '0.070'],
        ['Example scenario (22.5, 3.1, 80, 28)', '0.491', 'MODERATE', '0.508', '0.522', '0.535'],
        ['Storm surge (42, 4.5, 88, 26)', '0.942', 'CRITICAL (gauge rule also on)', '0.974', '0.990', '0.990'],
        ['Severe cloudburst (95, 7.2, 98, 23)', '0.850', 'CRITICAL (gauge rule also on)', '0.921', '0.978', '0.990']], widths=[4, 1, 2.6, 1.3, 1.3, 1.3], size=9)
    b.h2('4.6 Geospatial Methods')
    b.p('**Catchments.** Catchment boundaries are read from ESRI shapefiles by a native binary parser and converted to WGS84 GeoJSON for the map; the converted result is cached in memory so that map requests are served quickly. **Great-circle distance.** Distances between an incident and candidate squads, shelters or hospitals use the Haversine formula for latitude and longitude in radians with Earth radius R of 6,371 km (Equation 4.8). Only squads with status AVAILABLE and shelters with free capacity at least equal to the number of victims are considered, and the nearest candidate is proposed. Because great-circle distance ignores flooded roads, it is a ranking aid and not a route.')
    b.eq('d = 2R · atan2( √a, √(1 − a) ),   a = sin²(Δφ/2) + cos φ₁ · cos φ₂ · sin²(Δλ/2)', '4.8')
    b.p('**Beacon trilateration.** When a distress beacon packet has no GPS fix, its position can be estimated from the received signal strength at three or more gateways. The strength is converted to a distance with the log-distance path-loss model of Equation 4.9, where RSSI₀ is the reference strength at one metre and n the path-loss exponent, and the position is found by least squares after projecting the coordinates to a local plane. The beacon registry in the prototype is simulated, so this method is verified by unit tests and not by field measurements.')
    b.eq('d = 10 ^ ( (RSSI₀ − RSSI) / (10 · n) )', '4.9')
    b.h2('4.7 Incident Lifecycle, Approval Gate and Photo Analysis')
    b.p('An incident moves through the states REPORTED, UNDER VERIFICATION, VERIFIED, QUEUED, TRIAGE IN PROGRESS, PENDING APPROVAL, DISPATCHED, IN TRANSIT, ON SCENE and RESOLVED, with the side states DUPLICATE, REJECTED and CANCELLED. A table of allowed transitions is checked on the server for every change. The approval rule (Equation 4.10) is deterministic code that runs after any recommendation, so no software component, including an optional language model, can bypass it. Here V is the number of people affected and S the severity.')
    b.eq('RequiresApproval(I) = ( V ≥ 5 ) ∨ ( S ∈ {HIGH, CRITICAL} )', '4.10')
    b.p('To prevent the gate being weakened by editing a record, the rule uses the **effective risk**: the assessment recorded at creation is kept, and later edits can only raise it. An unknown severity counts as HIGH. Only a commander or administrator can authorise a downgrade in writing, which is recorded. A gated incident can reach DISPATCHED only through the approval route, and the squad status and the incident state are changed together in one database transaction.')
    b.p('**Photo analysis.** Each uploaded photo is validated for type, size and decoded-pixel limits, its EXIF data is read, and it is stored privately. Two pretrained models run through ONNX Runtime on the CPU: CLIP ViT-B/32 decides zero-shot between eight flood prompts and ten normal prompts (flood if the flood probability is at least 0.5), and SegFormer-B0 fine-tuned on ADE20K labels pixels to give water coverage and the people, vehicles and boats standing in water. The prompts and threshold were fixed before scoring. The output suggests a severity and is labelled PREDICTED; water depth is not estimated, because two single-photo methods tried did not separate shallow from deep water, so the reporter enters a depth band.')
    b.h2('4.8 Alert Rules')
    b.p('Alerts follow three rules. A new alert is a **duplicate** of an alert in force (status ACTIVE or ACKNOWLEDGED) when it has the same title, or the same severity within 1 km and within a 30-minute window; duplicates are not created again. An unacknowledged alert is **escalated** after 10 minutes for CRITICAL, 20 minutes for HIGH and 60 minutes for WARNING severity, up to two levels. Each alert can be exported as **Common Alerting Protocol (CAP) 1.2 XML** with area circle, severity and sender. Delivery to phones is not implemented; alerts are shown inside the application and exported for external systems.')
    b.h2('4.9 Authentication, Sessions and Multi-Factor Authentication')
    b.p('Passwords are hashed with PBKDF2-SHA256 using 240,000 iterations and a random salt. After a correct password, an account with MFA receives a single-use challenge valid for 300 seconds and no session. The second factor is a time-based one-time password (RFC 6238: HMAC-SHA1, six digits, 30-second steps) checked with a narrow drift window and step-level replay prevention, or a single-use recovery code (ten codes of 80 bits). Only then is a server-side session created. The browser receives a 15-minute access token that is held in memory and bound to the session, and a rotating single-use refresh token in an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie protected by a CSRF double-submit check and an Origin check. Sessions end on password change, role or team change, deactivation or administrator revocation, and idle sessions expire. TOTP secrets are stored encrypted with AES-256-GCM using environment keys. Sign-in, registration, verification, recovery and invitation endpoints are rate limited in a shared database store.')
    b.p('Privileged accounts (administrator, developer, commander, operator, rescue officer) cannot self-register. They are created by invitations that are hashed, single-use and expiring, or by a host-authorised bootstrap tool, and they must verify a contact address and enrol MFA before reaching the console. Citizens and volunteers may enable MFA voluntarily.')
    b.h2('4.10 Map Source Strategy')
    b.p('The map first tries the configured online base map. If three tile requests fail without a success, or nothing arrives within six seconds, it moves to a second online provider, then to an optional locally served regional vector archive, then to a small bundled map built from Natural Earth (public domain) and BBMP ward boundaries (CC BY-SA 2.5 India, from DataMeet), and finally shows an explicit notice. Overlays are drawn on every source, and a chip states which source is active. The bundled data is about 315 KB and holds ward polygons and names, highways, railways, rivers, lakes, urban extent, towns and airports. It does not contain street-level roads or buildings, which is stated in the documentation.')
